NAME
    Email::Barcode::Decode - decode barcodes in email attachments

SYNOPSIS
        use Email::Barcode::Decode;

        my $ebd = Email::Barcode::Decode->new(email => $raw_email_string);
        foreach my $symbol ($ebd->get_symbols) {
            print(
                'decoded '  . $symbol->{type} .
                ' symbol "' . $symbol->{data} .'"'.
                ' file "'   . $symbol->{filename} .'"'.
                "\n"
            );
        }

DESCRIPTION
    Email::Barcode::Decode finds barcodes and QR codes in the attachments of
    an email and returns their decoded content.

    The constructor saves every MIME part that has a filename to a temporary
    directory, including parts inside nested multiparts. When "pdftoppm" is
    installed, each page of a PDF attachment is rendered into a grayscale
    image instead of saving the PDF itself.

    "get_symbols" reads each saved file with Imager, converts it to 8-bit
    grayscale and scans it with Barcode::ZBar. With the default
    "@enhancers", each image is scanned twice: once in plain grayscale and
    once converted to pure black and white. Attachments that Imager cannot
    read, such as text files, are skipped.

REQUIREMENTS
    *   Imager with support for the image formats you expect. Imager reads
        JPEG, PNG, GIF and TIFF only if it was built with the matching
        libraries (Debian package "libimager-perl" includes them). Run "perl
        -MImager -e 'print join(q{ }, Imager->read_types), "\n"'" to see the
        formats your installation can read.

    *   Optional: "pdftoppm" from Poppler (Debian package "poppler-utils")
        to scan PDF attachments. Without it, PDF attachments are skipped.

ACCESSORS
  email
    The raw email string passed to "new".

  header_obj
    The Email::MIME::Header object of the email.

  attached_files
    Array reference of Path::Class::File objects, one for each saved
    attachment or rendered PDF page, in the order of the MIME parts.

    Each attachment is saved as "<tmpdir>/<part number>/<file name>". The
    file name is the attachment filename without any directory components,
    so a malicious filename such as "../../.bashrc" cannot write outside the
    temporary directory, and attachments with the same filename do not
    overwrite each other. Control characters are removed, and names longer
    than 200 bytes (UTF-8) are shortened, keeping the extension. A name that
    is empty, "." or ".." after this becomes "attachment".

    Rendered PDF pages are named after the PDF filename, with dots replaced
    by dashes: "scan.pdf" becomes "scan-pdf-page-1.pgm",
    "scan-pdf-page-2.pgm" and so on. Page numbers are zero-padded to equal
    width, so sorting the names gives the page order. Each page is an 8-bit
    grayscale image whose long side is "$MAX_HEIGHT" pixels, whatever the
    page size in the PDF.

    The temporary directory is removed when the program exits.

METHODS
  new
        my $ebd = Email::Barcode::Decode->new(email => $raw_email_string);

    Parses the email and saves its attachments as described in
    "attached_files". Croaks when "email" is missing or empty.

    When "pdftoppm" fails, for example on a damaged or password-protected
    PDF, "new" warns with the exit status or signal and the "pdftoppm" error
    message, and keeps any pages that were rendered:

        pdftoppm failed for scan.pdf (exit 1): Syntax Error: Couldn't find trailer dictionary ...

    The warning is plain ASCII: non-ASCII characters are transliterated with
    Text::Unidecode, so "ärger.pdf" is reported as "arger.pdf".

  get_symbols
    Scans all "attached_files" and returns a list of hash references, one
    per decoded symbol:

        (
            {
                filename => 'vcard-pdf-page-2.pgm',
                type     => 'QR-Code',
                data     => 'http://search.cpan.org/perldoc?Email%3A%3ABarcode%3A%3ADecode',
            },
        )

    "filename"
        Base name of the scanned file in "attached_files".

    "type"
        Symbol type as reported by Barcode::ZBar, for example "QR-Code",
        "CODE-128" or "EAN-13".

    "data"
        Decoded content of the symbol.

    Symbols are returned in the order of "attached_files". Within one file,
    the same "data" is returned only once, even when several enhancers find
    it. The same content in different files is returned once per file.

    Dies with "raster size mismatch" when an enhancer returns a raster whose
    length is not "width * height"; see "@enhancers".

  email_name
    Returns the display name of the first "From" address, falling back to
    the local part of the address when there is no display name. Returns
    "undef", or an empty list in list context, when the email has no "From"
    header or its first address is not a valid address, such as "<>".

  email_from
    Returns the first "From" address, for example "foo@example.com". Returns
    "undef", or an empty list in list context, in the same cases as
    "email_name".

CONFIGURATION
    These package variables control how attachments are converted and
    scanned. Localize them around the call that reads them to limit the
    change to your code:

        local $Email::Barcode::Decode::MAX_HEIGHT = 2000;

  $MAX_HEIGHT
    Images taller than this are scaled down to this height, keeping the
    aspect ratio, before scanning. Default: 1500 pixels, because larger
    images may not decode well with Barcode::ZBar. PDF pages are rendered
    with their long side at this size, which also bounds the disk space a
    PDF can take. Read by "new" and "get_symbols".

  $MAX_PDF_PAGES
    Number of pages rendered per PDF attachment; later pages are ignored.
    Default: 237. Read by "new".

  @enhancers
    List of code references. "get_symbols" calls each of them for every
    image and scans each result.

    Each enhancer receives the Imager object of the image and must return
    "($raw, 'Y800', $width, $height)", where $raw is an 8-bit grayscale
    raster of exactly "$width * $height" bytes. The enhancer must not modify
    the Imager object in place, because all enhancers receive the same
    object.

    The default enhancers return:

    1. the plain grayscale image, leaving binarization to ZBar;
    2. the image converted to pure black and white with a fixed threshold of
    128.

    Both turn transparent areas white, as barcodes are usually dark on a
    light or transparent background.

SEE ALSO
    Barcode::ZBar, Imager, Email::MIME, pdftoppm(1)

